Primary driver: Pichowicz W, Kotas M, Kalka N, Dembowska J, Karska J, Piotrowski P, Misiak B.
"Responses of AI chatbots to escalating suicide risk: A simulation study of repeated interactions."
J Affect Disord 2026;122586. doi 10.1016/j.jad.2026.122586, PMID 42833407.
**Peer-reviewed and PubMed-verified 8 Oct 2026** — the only peer-reviewed item in this week's
intake. Department of Psychiatry, Wroclaw Medical University.

Design: simulated interactions with ChatGPT, DeepSeek and Replika across three suicidal-risk
scenarios ESCALATING over seven days, informed by the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale.
Outcomes: exploration of suicidal ideation, symptom assessment, referral to human support, and
vulnerability to jailbreak attempts. 27 trajectories.

Findings that carry:
  - Overall human referral: 54/63 (85.7%) ChatGPT, 48/63 (76.2%) DeepSeek, 6/63 (9.5%) Replika.
  - Day-7 (imminent-risk) referral: 9/9, 4/9, 1/9 trajectories respectively.
  - **Jailbreaking succeeded in 6/9 (95% CI 29.9-92.5%), 7/9 (40.0-97.2%) and 8/9 (51.8-99.7%)
    attempts** respectively — i.e. in the majority of attempts against every system tested,
    including the one with the best unprompted referral rate.
  - Authors' own limits, reproduced rather than smoothed: "The simulation design and small sample
    of 27 trajectories limit generalizability."

================================================================================
EDIT 1 — BOUNDARY. Separate safeguard ACTIVATION from safeguard ROBUSTNESS
Sections affected: §10 (what this study does not establish); Table A4 (optional companion hunk)

The gap in OUR protocol. The exploratory endpoint is "**Crisis-safeguard activation consistency
across matched suicidality presentations**" (§2 objective 3), and Table A4 defines its ascertainment
as "Safeguard-language detection on matched suicidality presentations, physician-adjudicated; never
jury-scored." That measures whether the safeguard FIRES on a presentation. It does not measure
whether the safeguard HOLDS when a user pushes against it.

Those are different properties, and the distinction is not pedantic: a safeguard that activates
reliably and can then be talked out of it in two-thirds to seven-eighths of attempts has not been
shown to protect anyone. Our instrument, as written, would score such a system as having consistent
crisis-safeguard activation — which would be true, and misleading.

Suicidality is the highest-harm domain the protocol touches (Appendix B lists acute suicidality
under psychiatric/toxicologic scenarios), so this is the worst place to leave the activation /
robustness distinction implicit.

Scope discipline — what this edit does NOT do. It adds **no** adversarial prompting, no jailbreak
probes, no scenario variants, no participant burden, and has **no bearing on the §8.4 power
calculation**. It is a disclosure of what an activation measurement does and does not establish.
Building an actual robustness probe would be a design expansion; that option is written up
separately below and is deliberately NOT proposed.

Honesty about transfer: Pichowicz et al. is a simulation study of 27 trajectories against three
consumer systems, two of which (DeepSeek, Replika) this protocol does not test; Replika is a
companion app, not a triage system, and carries the worst numbers. The proposed text therefore
cites the bypass finding as an established *property of the class* requiring disclosure, and does
not import the rates as estimates for our systems.

