1. NINE OF THIS WEEK'S TEN NEW REFERENCES ARE PREPRINTS. None is cited in any proposed edit. At
   this project's citation bar a preprint can corroborate but cannot carry a protocol change, and
   several of these are single-author or unverifiable. Listed with what each would have supported:
   - "LLMs Anchor on Chief Complaint and Fail to Integrate Evidence in Sequential Clinical Triage"
     (arXiv 10.48550/arXiv.2609.22904, design_overlap true) — six LLMs predicting ESI from a growing
     conversation prefix; labels anchored on the early chief-complaint exchanges at every checkpoint
     and **prompting interventions did not lift the plateau**; expert clinicians QWK 0.887-0.929 vs
     0.295 for the best model. Would have borne on Change 12 and on the Anchoring Failure Rate
     endpoint, and it sits in tension with BiasMedQA (10 Sep patch), where debiasing prompts DID
     reduce bias. **That tension is worth watching**: if it survives peer review it complicates the
     prompt-sensitivity claim in the pending 10 Sep EDIT 2. Nurse-patient (clinician-facing) ESI,
     not lay register.
   - "LLMs for Medical Consultation Are Evaluated Too Late: The Preformulation Gap"
     (arXiv 10.48550/arXiv.2608.17330) — benchmarks begin after the problem is already framed;
     self-care advice before any patient answer in 9/12 baseline cells vs 0/12 instruction cells.
     Would have supported the lay-register rationale and the pending 17 Sep single-turn boundary.
   - "The complexities of patient-centred conversational artificial intelligence" (arXiv) —
     2,053 real patient-chatbot conversations, 1,164 clinician-graded cases, finding that
     **communication style significantly alters triage outcomes**. This is the single best match to
     our dual-register premise to appear so far. **It has NO DOI and NO PMID and is
     websearch-verified only — quarantined, cited nowhere.** Worth the PI's attention if it reaches
     a journal.
   - Geographic-language disparity (arXiv 2606.01204) and socioeconomic/ZIP-code disparity
     (arXiv 2607.22605) — both single-author probes of one or three models. Fairness-adjacent;
     ancillary.
   - PatientAgentBench (arXiv 2607.25485); "Are Frontier LLMs Safer Than Government-Backed Symptom
     Checkers" (medRxiv 10.64898/2026.09.01.26361908); MedSafe-Dx v0 (medRxiv
     10.64898/2026.04.14.26350711) — benchmark-methodology items, library only.

2. "Triage and Referral Behavior Across Patient-Facing Medical AI Products" (medRxiv
   10.64898/2026.08.15.26360518, UCLA emergency medicine, design_overlap true) — **OUT OF SCOPE BY
   DESIGN, and worth saying why.** Nine patient-facing products over 540 multi-turn encounters on
   60 physician-developed cases: triage accuracy did not differ by product category, but branded
   health-AI products over-triaged low-acuity cases far more (28% vs 3% vs 2%) and more often
   recommended **affiliated, fee-requiring services**. That is a commercial self-referral failure
   mode, and this protocol's §5.5 Arm A tests "each frontier model" — general-purpose systems with
   no affiliated services to steer toward. The mechanism has no referent in our design. It is a
   strong successor-study hypothesis and nothing more; proposing a referral-destination endpoint
   here would add an axis the systems under test cannot exhibit.

